Apply morphisms as f.T(x), not f(x).

A morphism is a record of functions, one per member type. Bare f(x)
does not say which type is mapped, and overloads function application
with morphism application. The type-component spelling f.T(x) is
already in the grammar; T is looked up in the body of the model f is
a morphism of, not on type Mor(M). Nested paths such as f.N.T(x) are
rejected until nested models exist.

f(x) remains function application. Morphism application as f(x) was
never released, so there is no migration diagnostic.
